## Where I am

- `bank-api` is a finished-with learning exercise. I am moving on to a new
  project: a **game ladder / tournament API** (players, matches, ratings,
  leaderboard). Angular frontend comes after the API works.
- Goal: get hired as a junior Java developer, so the new project should be
  portfolio-quality — real database, validation, tests, deployed.

## What I am comfortable with

- Java basics; Spring Boot project layout.
- `@RestController`, `@GetMapping`, `@PathVariable`.
- Constructor injection and why Spring beans are singletons.
- Splitting code into controller / service / repository layers, and coding
  against a repository interface rather than an implementation.
- Stereotype annotations (`@Component` / `@Service` / `@Repository`) and why
  putting one on an interface does nothing.

## What I have not covered yet

- Persistence: JPA, `@Entity`, Spring Data repositories, a real database.
- POST / PUT / DELETE handlers, `@RequestBody`.
- DTOs and request validation (`@Valid`).
- Exception handling and choosing HTTP status codes; `ResponseEntity`.
- Writing tests of any kind.
- WebSockets, Docker, deployment.

## Habits I am still working on

- Defaulting fields and methods to `private`; not exposing mutable
  collections.
- Keeping read methods free of side effects.
- Not letting one class take on two responsibilities (e.g. storage plus
  seed data).

## How to help me

- See my user rules: hints and explanations, not finished code.
- Explain the reasoning behind conventions, not just the convention.
